  "content": {
    "id": "p18-q22-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "22",
    "prompt": "Find all solutions $a,b\\in\\mathbb R$ to $\\frac1{a+b}=\\frac1a+\\frac1b$, or explain why no solutions exist.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "No real solutions.\n\nFor $b\\ne0$, the resulting quadratic in $a$ has discriminant $-3b^2<0$.",
      "worked": "The denominators require $a\\ne0$, $b\\ne0$ and $a+b\\ne0$. Multiplying by $ab(a+b)$ gives\n$$\\begin{align*}ab&=b(a+b)+a(a+b)\\\\ab&=a^2+2ab+b^2\\\\a^2+ab+b^2&=0.\\end{align*}$$\nAs a quadratic in $a$,\n$$\\begin{align*}\\Delta&=b^2-4(1)(b^2)\\\\\\Delta&=-3b^2<0.\\end{align*}$$\nSince $b\\ne0$, there are no real solutions.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 68
  },
